    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Table 1: Physical parameters and numerical values of the three-element Windkessel parameters for every outlet."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 49,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 70.0, 492.333, 541.667, 524.667 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "a semi-implicit 3D-0D coupling scheme as seen in [9]. The full algorithm is detailed in the appendix."
  }, {
    "type" : "heading",
    "pdfua_tag" : "H1",
    "id" : 50,
    "level" : "Doctitle",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 71.0, 462.0, 257.667, 479.0 ],
    "heading level" : 1,
    "font" : "CMBX12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "3.1.2 Synthetic measurements"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 51,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 69.667, 381.667, 542.0, 456.333 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The forward solution is generated with a time step of dt = 1 ms and undersampled in time to dt meas = 15 ms , leading to a total of 56 measurements. From the solution of the forward problem, we simulate a PC-MRI acquisition by subsampling into a rectangular measurement mesh with a resolution of [2 mm, 2 mm, 2 mm ] and then applying the process described in Section 2.1 with a venc of double the maximal velocity. The magnitude is modelled as"
  }, {
    "type" : "formula",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Formula",
    "id" : 52,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 233.667, 341.667, 541.0, 371.667 ],
    "content" : "1.0 if z is in the lumen of the vessel (16) 0.5 otherwise."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 53,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 70.0, 299.667, 540.0, 330.0 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Finally a complex Gaussian noise ϵ ∈ C N is added with a signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) of 15. Fifty independent realizations of the noise were generated."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 54,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 69.667, 197.667, 542.0, 300.0 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "For comparison, we reconstructed velocity measurements from these synthetic measurements using the Berkeley Advanced Reconstruction Toolbox (BART)[11]. BART is a command-line-based software that provides a flexible framework of compressed sensing methods, as well as tools for simulation, pre-processing, and image reconstruction, providing a multitude of different regularization options. In this work, we have used this toolbox for compressed sensing reconstructions of the velocities, using total variation in time as for the regularization."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 55,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 70.333, 96.043, 542.023, 199.333 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Next, the sampling mask is applied to these simulated frequency space measurements. We take a 2D subsampled mask in the x − y -plane and sample fully in the z -direction as in [4]. We consider different subsampling rates R = N sampled N total = 8 , 16 , 32, with two different masks: the pseudo-spiral mask and the pseudo-random Gaussian mask, which is sampled according to a Gaussian probability distribution, as shown in Figure 2. For the pseudo-spiral mask, the points are placed evenly on a cartesian grid along a spiral with six turns and a final radius reaching the edge of the mask."
  } ]
}